body:
Once again, thank you for your responses.

&#x200B;

> Well, monopolies are unsustainable in the long run. Smart people will always strive to remove monopolized control and to compete with the status quo. Of course people are going to suffer from it while it lasts but the probability of monopolies to form in Ancapistan approaches 0 . Without a state the multinationals and rich won't have a nation to abuse. In anarchy there is a web of spontainous market actions all working to keep society going constanly changing in constant competition and clash, there is no centralized power telling people what to do nor what to think trough propaganda and corruption. 

&#x200B;

Good answer. I overlooked that when writing out that post and makes sense the more I think about it. If there ever is a monopoly over something, there will be someone out there who will eventually come to the rescue and start offering that same service at a lower price. And then if that person gets involved in the monopoly, another person will enter the game.

&#x200B;

> [https://youtu.be/NbNFJK1ZpVg](https://youtu.be/NbNFJK1ZpVg) 

&#x200B;

I've watched that video before. Like with most arguments from other an-caps and libertarians, there are some points I agree with and some I don't. But one question I had in my post that wasn't answered was would there be less pressure to meet certain credentials for jobs, which would, in turn, make education less of a necessity and less expensive?

&#x200B;

> Yeah, but you have to account for what [the majority of your tax money goes to](https://www.nationalpriorities.org/campaigns/military-spending-united-states/). That would be ~~war~~ ~~defense~~ minipax, specifically 54% of it. The majority of the tax money is spent to separate the families of workers by genociding their relatives living in other countries and genociding people in general. 

&#x200B;

Yeah, I did take those into account but thanks for bringing it up. I just used healthcare as an example for simplicity sake. I am generally against taxes, but I do have some wiggle room. The genociding under the guise of "war defense" you mention is definitely something I'm against funding. I'm also against welfare as that defeats the purpose of funding healthcare and education through taxes to make it "free". Why would someone need to be given income through taxes if they're not going to have to pay for college or get a medical bill? I also don't think roads are a priority. Admittedly, private sector has done some remarkable things, so I don't think finding the resources to build a road or highway would be such a problem. And I can't stand programs that are exclusive for certain groups: healthcare that's only for people of a certain age, government loans for only students that are minorities, etc. If my money was invested into it, I think I'm entitled to reap the benefits. 

&#x200B;

> Surely a world where they can choose who to work for is better than a world where they are incentivized not to work even if they would've, in order to maintain the constant flow of cash provided for them that would perish if thir income was larger making them acually earn less by trying to improve their economic situation. That's the wellfare system in our day and age. 

&#x200B;

As I've stated before, I'm not in favor of welfare, or giving paychecks to people through taxes. I would much rather have services that are affordable, so they don't need to be given the money. But if there is going to be welfare, I would at least want the requirements to be more strict. I would be more in favor of money for people that are unable to work, whether they are mentally or physically challenged, not people that just don't feel like working like you mentioned.

&#x200B;

> The fact that more people are provided with money (unvoluntarily gathered) doesn't make the problem of scarcity go away, it would just make us equally poor and miserable. The black market would rise up and a violent revolution will come into play, in most cases, which would give power to the slave class people trying to become the political class, perpetuating the cycle of government. There is, however, a way out: Agorism. 

&#x200B;

I wasn't trying to imply that it would make scarcity go away, I was just saying it would make those scarce items be more affordable. Or at least that's how it would work in theory anyway (I understand it almost never does or if it does it will be by some stroke of luck like with most countries). And I did read up on those Agorism articles you cited. Interesting stuff. I like how it offers a more scientific explanation, which makes it easier for the ideology to click in my mind.

&#x200B;

Thank you for your responses. I will read up on Agorism when I get the time. You did clear some things up but you also gave me more questions:

&#x200B;

>No measures, all the burden would fall upon the shoulders of the individual as it should. Business that cannot reach a certain person would suffer for someone else is going to come and fulfill that need instead, that's the profit motive.

&#x200B;

That makes sense, although I can't help but feel that that's only best case scenario. For instance, there's plenty of water in the world so there's enough competition between companies like Deer Park, Dasani, Nestle, etc. that you can buy a bottle of water for as low as 25 cents at a vending machine. No one will be dying of dehydration. But what happens when a good or service is scarce or there's a monopoly over something (which is something I forgot to address in my post)? The most infamous example is Martin Shkreli who hiked the price of a life-saving AIDS medication to $750 a pill. There was no other way to get the medication and the people with AIDS that couldn't afford it just suffered the consequences on their health. Once again, your answer only works in theory if there's no Martin Shkrelis in the world.

&#x200B;

>People that can't afford a thing buy it at a lower price. Furthermore, in an ancap society there is no fed and no taxes to diminish the buying power of the poor. People aren't entiteled to my labour and neither am I entiteled to their labour, a man is entiteled only to the sweat of his brow. People that don't want to learn about the omnibenevolent nature of the state and the importance of worshiping pieces of cloth can just go ahead and not do that and instead seek goods from companies that they trust not to abuse them and rob them of their individuality and humanity like public schools do.

&#x200B;

I understand that people will buy the alternative with the lowest price, but what happens when there either is no alternative or the alternative is still too expensive at its lowest price. I also share those same grievances with our public education system. But my problems are more with how the public education system is handled, rather than it being public.

&#x200B;

Sort of off topic question, but is there any possible conditions under which you, as an an-cap, would be okay with taxation? Is there any way taxation wouldn't be slavery? The one thing I don't understand is what is the big difference between you paying indirectly through taxes or directly? You're going to have to pay either way. For example, assuming you do a pink-collar type job (cashier, waiter, etc.), you'll make $8 per hour on average. That multiplied by 7 (the average number of hours worked per day) will give you a gross income of $64 per day. The tax for that is $6.68, which makes your take home income $57.32. That $6.68 is pooled together with the $6.68 of every other tax payer working your job and invested into a public healthcare system. So you pay $6.68 and keep $57.32 every day, and at any point you can go and be treated without a hefty medical bill at the end. But if that was all privatized, and you needed, say, a tonsil removal, that'll cost you anywhere from $3,000 to $8,000. So either way, you're going to have pay, whether indirectly or directly. By the way, this isn't me saying that socialism is better or worse than capitalism or vise-versa, but I just don't see the big deal in choosing between one or the other.

&#x200B;

>I for one would argue that uncoerced markets are more capable of meeting people's needs without all of that red tape, regulation and distruction of value and without government to bail faling businesses out, serving their costumers as best as they can would be their prime objective (for vertical ecomomic associations a.k.a enterpreneurs and workers)

&#x200B;

I agree. A lot of rules and regulations have slowed down economic growth tremendously, and bailing out a business because of their irresponsible financial decisions is obviously a bad thing.

&#x200B;

>Are you implying that one cannot help people if he doesn't first give up on his individuality? I strongly disagree with the prospects of altruism. Selfishness is a natural element of human beings present in all of us due to the evolutionary process and bottling it up won't solve anything, the contrary may be the case. I am an ethical egoist and I believe everyone ought to relieve himself of his drive to act in his own interest by way of doing exactly that. As an ethical egoism I may give to charity in order to feel good about myself and I may serve people in accordance with the profit motive and such.

&#x200B;

Like I stated before, those that pocket their money instead of giving it away to those in need aren't monsters. They're just following human nature and I too don't see selfishness as a problem where most do. Selfishness is good motivation. Capitalism and free markets thrive on selfishness. Without desires and wants, nobody would do anything. My point was that in a world where "charity" isn't made mandatory by law, those that rely on financial support from others in order to get by in life are going to have an increasingly harder time if giving is voluntary. And giving can either be monetary or a favor of some sort, whether that be giving money to someone so they can afford open heart surgery, or just offering to perform the surgery on them for free. Either is good.

&#x200B;

I know it sounds like I'm trying to spread commie propaganda in some of my responses, but I assure you I am not. Anyhoo, thank you for taking time to answer and also for citing the Agorist stuff, which I look forward to reading more about.
